course: religion-ethics
lesson: 15
title: Parables: the Talents, the Sower and more
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In the Talents a master gives his servants large sums of money; two trade and double theirs, but one buries his out of fear — Christians read it as «use the gifts God gives you, do not hide them».
explain: In the Sower seed falls on the path, on rocky ground, among thorns and on good soil; Jesus explained the soils as kinds of listener, and only the good soil gives a big harvest.
explain: Short parables picture God's kingdom: a tiny mustard seed that grows into a large plant where birds nest, a pearl so precious a merchant sells everything for it; and the two builders show that a house on rock stands in the storm while one on sand falls.
explain: Others are about money and mercy: the unforgiving servant is let off a huge debt but will not forgive a small one; the rich fool builds bigger barns and dies that night; vineyard workers hired late are paid the same as those hired early.
explain: For a child today: try instead of hiding, listen with an open heart, forgive as you were forgiven, and remember life is not about how much you own; the vineyard story is a puzzle people still argue about.
fact: In the Parable of the Talents a talent was a large sum of money; two servants traded and doubled theirs, and one buried his.
fact: The servant who buried his talent said he was afraid of his master.
fact: In the Parable of the Sower seed fell on the path, on rocky ground, among thorns and on good soil.
fact: Jesus explained the four soils as kinds of listener; only the good soil gave a big harvest.
fact: Seed on the rocky ground sprang up fast but withered in the sun because it had no deep roots.
fact: Jesus said the thorns stand for worries and the love of riches, which choke the good word like weeds.
fact: The kingdom of God is like a tiny mustard seed that grows into a large plant where birds nest.
fact: In the pearl of great price a merchant sold everything he had to buy one precious pearl.
fact: A wise man built his house on rock and a foolish man on sand; when the storm came, only the house on rock stood.
fact: The two builders show that those who put Jesus' teaching into practice stand firm in hard times.
fact: The unforgiving servant was forgiven a huge debt but refused to forgive a small debt owed to him.
fact: In the workers in the vineyard, workers hired late in the day were paid the same as those hired early.
fact: The early workers were paid exactly what the landowner had promised them in the morning, yet they grumbled.
fact: Christians read the vineyard parable as showing that God's generosity is not earned, but many find it puzzling and its fairness is discussed.
fact: The rich fool built bigger barns to store his wealth, but died that night.
fact: Jesus said the rich fool should have been «rich towards God», not just storing things for himself.
exercise: What was a talent in Jesus' parable? | A large sum of money | A special skill like singing | A kind of farm animal | A small silver coin
exercise: What did the first two servants do with their talents? | Traded and doubled them | Buried them in the ground | Spent them on a feast | Gave them back at once
exercise: Why did the third servant bury his talent? | He was afraid of his master | He wanted to plant it like a seed | He forgot where his master lived | He wanted to give it to the poor
exercise: What do Christians learn from the buried talent? | Do not let fear of failing stop you using your gifts | Always keep your money hidden somewhere it is safe from thieves | Never take risks with anything you own | Gifts should be kept secret from others
exercise: Where did the sower's seed fall? | On the path, rocks, thorns and good soil | Only on good soil, planted in neat straight rows | In the river, the sea and the village well | On the roof, the road and the busy market
exercise: Which soil gave a big harvest? | The good soil | The rocky ground | The path | The thorny ground
exercise: What did Jesus say the four soils stand for? | Different kinds of listener | Four countries of the world | The four seasons of the year | Four tribes of Israel
exercise: What is the kingdom of God compared to in the mustard seed? | A tiny seed that grows into a large plant | A huge tree that is cut down | A seed that never grows at all, however much it is watered | A field that floods each spring
exercise: What did the merchant do to get the pearl of great price? | Sold everything he had | Stole it from the king | Found it on the beach for free | Swapped it for one sheep
exercise: Which house stood in the storm? | The house built on rock | The house built on sand | Both houses fell down | Both houses stood firm
exercise: What does building on rock stand for? | Putting Jesus' teaching into practice | Living in a strong stone house of your own | Moving up to the mountains where it is safe | Becoming a builder or a mason when you grow up
exercise: What did the unforgiving servant do wrong? | Refused to forgive a small debt after being forgiven a huge one | Stole money from his master's house | Forgave everyone too quickly | Buried his master's talent in the ground
exercise: What was surprising about the workers in the vineyard? | Late workers were paid the same as early ones | Early workers were paid double what the late ones got | Nobody was paid anything at all | Only the youngest workers were paid
exercise: What did the rich fool do with his wealth? | Built bigger barns to store it all | Shared it out among the poor of the village | Lost it all in a storm at sea on his ship | Gave it all to his younger son to look after
exercise: What happened to the rich fool? | He died that same night | He lived to a great age | He became king of the land | He lost everything in a fire
exercise: What do Christians say the vineyard parable shows about God? | His generosity is not earned | He pays only those who work longest | He prefers workers who arrive first | He forgets to pay some workers
test: Sam: «the moral of the Talents is just keep ur money safe». Put him right. | No, it praises using your gifts, not hiding them in fear | Yes, the servant who hid it was praised | Yes, burying money is the safest choice | No, it says money is evil
test: You're scared to try out for the school play in case you're rubbish. Which parable speaks to that? | The buried talent: fear can stop us doing anything | The rich fool: it is wrong to ever store anything up for later | The lost coin: always search the house | The pearl: sell everything you own
test: kid in Sunday school: «seed on rocks grew fast then died, so rocks are best lol». right? | No, it withered for lack of roots; good soil was best | Yes, the rocky ground gave the biggest harvest | Yes, all four soils gave the same harvest | No, nothing ever grew on the rocks
test: Your mate lets you off owing him a fiver, then you hound your little brother over 20p. Which parable? | The unforgiving servant | The two builders | The mustard seed | The pearl of great price
test: Buddhist classmate: «What's the point of the mustard seed story?» Best answer? | Small beginnings can grow into something great | Mustard is the best plant to grow | Seeds should never be planted in gardens, only in fields | Birds are more important than plants
test: Judge: «Was the landowner in the vineyard fair? Give both sides.» Best-reasoned answer? | Early workers got the promised pay, but it felt unfair; the story is about generosity | It was fair, because the Bible says so, end of | It was unfair, so the story is just stupid | The late workers were lazy so nobody should have paid them
test: «Life's about getting loads of stuff, then u can chill». Which parable answers that? | The rich fool, who died the night he stored it all | The good soil, which gave a big harvest | The two builders, who built two houses | The lost sheep, who wandered off and was carried home again
test: Grandad says faith is like building on rock. What does he mean? | Living by Jesus' teaching keeps you steady in hard times | Churches should always be built of stone, never of wood or brick | You should never go to the beach | Rich people build better houses
test: Which parable shows someone giving up everything for one treasure? | The pearl of great price | The rich fool and his barns | The buried talent in the ground | The four soils of the sower
test: A judge asks what «thorns» could choke good things in a child's life today. Best answer? | Worries and distractions like screens crowding out good habits | Real thorn bushes in the school field | Teachers who give too much homework to anyone | Nothing, the thorns were only about farming
test: «If the late workers got the same pay, why work hard at all?» Best-reasoned reply? | The story is about God's generosity, not a rule for wages; work still matters | You shouldn't, it proves hard work is pointless | Because the boss will always pay you double | It's fair because the late ones were richer
exam: Identify the reason given by the servant who concealed his talent in the earth. | That he was afraid of his master | That he wished to plant it for increase | That he intended it for the poor | That he had forgotten its whereabouts
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the significance of the four soils in the Parable of the Sower? | They represent different dispositions of those who hear | They represent the four seasons of the agricultural year | They represent four nations of the ancient world | They represent the four Gospels of the New Testament
exam: It is given that a servant was released from a debt of great magnitude. Ascertain the conduct for which he was subsequently condemned. | His refusal to forgive a lesser debt owed to himself | His failure to double the sum of money entrusted to his care | His construction of a dwelling upon sand | His expenditure of his inheritance abroad
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the parable of the rich fool. | He enlarged his barns to store his wealth and died that same night | He distributed his wealth to the poor and lived long | He forfeited his wealth through shipwreck | He bequeathed his barns to the Temple
